  "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $D(R)$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of $D(R)$ is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\u2026,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\u2026,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $D(R)$  of size $n,$ which imples that $D(R)$ cannot have finite dimension.     .\n\n- ${R}^{100}$ and ${R}_{2000}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{100})=100\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{2000}[t])=2000+1=2\\,001,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
